From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel <daniel@matv.io>
Date: Wed, 13 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] swstats_cpu: centre the AF sharpness window in PIXELS on all
 formats (packed + unpacked)

LOW-CONTRAST AF ROBUSTNESS + WINDOW MIS-PLACEMENT (OURS; companion to
the CDAF sweep-quality work in patches 07-10, 14). The soft-ISP CDAF
scores focus from the green squared-gradient metric
`stats_.sharpness += 1000 * Sum((g - g0)^2) / sumG`, accumulated only
inside a window predicate (patch 08). As shipped that predicate was
`(5*x)/window_.width == 3 && (5*y)/window_.height == 3`, which is true
for x,y in [0.6, 0.8) of the frame -- the FOURTH fifth on each axis, a
single 20%x20% = 4% patch sitting in the lower-right quadrant, NOT the
centre (the centred fifth is index 2, [0.4, 0.6)). The in-code comments
elsewhere call this "centre of picture", but the arithmetic never was.

Two consequences, both seen in the field on a ~1-2 m indoor shot of flat
white surfaces (washing machine / white cabinets / tiles) that locked at
the wrong distance and came out uniformly soft:

  1. Off-centre metering: the 4% patch samples the lower-right of the
     frame, so a subject the user framed centrally (where they aim) may
     fall entirely outside the AF window while off-axis clutter drives
     the lock.

  2. Tiny sampling area starves the metric on low-texture scenes. Over a
     4% patch of near-flat white the squared-gradient sum is a handful of
     sensor-noise counts, and dividing by the LARGE green sum of a bright
     white field drives it to near zero with high relative variance -- so
     the argmax over the ~21 coarse sweep positions is noise-driven and
     the lens lands at an arbitrary distance (the observed failure).

Aim: sample the centred middle third on each axis (x,y in [1/3, 2/3)),
which re-centres onto where the photographer aims and grows the window
from 4% to ~11% of the frame (~2.8x the linear extent, ~9x the pixels),
lifting the metric's signal-to-noise on marginally-textured scenes.

BUT the naive ratio test `(3*x)/window_.width == 1` is only correct where
`x` is a PIXEL index. The window predicate lives in a shared macro
(SWSTATS_ACCUMULATE_LINE_STATS) expanded by every per-format line-stat
function, and `x` does NOT have uniform units:

  - UNPACKED  statsBGGR{8,10,12}Line0: `x` is a pixel column index,
    loop `for (x = 0; x < window_.width; x += 4)`. window_.width is in
    pixels (debayer_cpu.cpp setWindow: window_.width = outputCfg.size
    .width), so `(3*x)/window_.width` is a genuine pixel ratio. OK.

  - PACKED    statsBGGR10PLine0 / statsGBRG10PLine0 (CSI-2 10-bit, 4
    pixels packed into 5 bytes): `x` is a BYTE offset, loop
    `for (x = 0; x < window_.width*5/4; x += 5)`. Here `x` runs to
    ~window_.width * 5/4 while window_.width is still in PIXELS, so
    `(3*x)/window_.width == 1` selects source columns ~26.7%-53.3% --
    still OFF-CENTRE. This is the format fajita actually runs: our
    sensors deliver packed pRAA (SRGGB10_CSI2P -> statsGBRG10PLine0) and
    pBAA (SBGGR10_CSI2P -> statsBGGR10PLine0), so on-device the ratio
    trick never centred the window at all.

Fix: make the window bounds map to the SAME pixels in every format by
testing against the loop's own x-domain extent rather than a hard-coded
pixel width. Each function already knows (or trivially derives) its x
upper bound; expose it as a uniformly-named local `windowXEnd`
(= window_.width for unpacked, = window_.width*5/4 for packed -- the
latter just renames the pre-existing `widthInBytes`) and use it both as
the loop bound and in the predicate:

    x >= windowXEnd / 3 && x < 2 * windowXEnd / 3

Because windowXEnd is expressed in the SAME units as `x` in each
function (pixels for unpacked, bytes for packed) and is linearly
proportional to the window width in pixels, [windowXEnd/3, 2*windowXEnd/3)
is the centred middle third IN PIXELS on every path. The packed loop
still steps by 5 bytes, so no 5-byte/4-pixel group is ever split by the
new bounds. `y` is a pixel line index in all functions (it is the frame
line index passed by processBayerFrame2, 0 <= y < window_.height in
pixels), so its test stays the pixel-domain `(3*y)/window_.height == 1`.

No per-pixel division is added to the hot loop -- windowXEnd/3 and
2*windowXEnd/3 are loop-invariant and the compiler hoists them; this is
cheaper than the old `(N*x)/window_.width` divide that ran every sample.

The metric stays exactly ratiometric: `sharpness` and its per-line
normaliser `sumG` both scale with the window, and the AF logic only ever
compares sharpness against its own running peak (patches 07-10, 14), so
no threshold or sweep constant needs retuning. No new state, no
allocation, no change to the squared-gradient kernel or normalisation.

HONEST LIMIT: this cannot rescue a genuinely textureless target.
Contrast-detection AF has zero gradient to hill-climb on a perfectly
flat field regardless of window size; a bigger centred window only helps
when SOME texture exists in frame (as in the kitchen scene). True
flat-white focus needs phase-detect / hybrid AF hardware this
contrast-only sensor does not have. Follow-ups that need on-device
tuning (multi-zone weighted window, low-contrast hold-position guard)
are deliberately left out of this minimal, unambiguous change.
